@startuml Decorator-Structure
skinparam classAttributeIconSize 0
title Decorator — pattern structure (Design Patterns, p. 177)

class Client

abstract class Component {
  + operation() : void
}
note right of Component
  Declares the interface for objects
  that can have responsibilities
  added to them dynamically.
  Keep it LIGHTWEIGHT (p. 180):
  every decorator pays for whatever
  a Component carries.
end note

class ConcreteComponent {
  + operation() : void
}
note bottom of ConcreteComponent
  The object being decorated.
  It knows nothing about its
  decorators, and never changes
  when a new one is invented.
end note

abstract class Decorator {
  - component : Component
  + operation() : void
}
note right of Decorator
  IS-A Component, so it can stand
  anywhere one can — including
  inside another Decorator.
  HAS-A Component, and forwards
  to it, so by default it changes
  nothing.
  Can be omitted when there is only
  one responsibility to add (p. 179).
end note

class ConcreteDecoratorA {
  - addedState : State
  + operation() : void
}

class ConcreteDecoratorB {
  + operation() : void
  + addedBehavior() : void
}
note bottom of ConcreteDecoratorB
  operation() calls
  super.operation() and adds
  its own work before, after,
  instead of, or several times.
end note

Client ..> Component : uses
Component <|-- ConcreteComponent
Component <|-- Decorator
Decorator o--> "component" Component : forwards to
Decorator <|-- ConcreteDecoratorA
Decorator <|-- ConcreteDecoratorB

note as N1
  The two relationships from Decorator to Component
  are what make the pattern work:
  **inheritance** buys interface conformance,
  **composition** buys run-time assembly.
  Remove either one and the pattern is gone.
end note
N1 .. Decorator

@enduml
